Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Aegis**, a colossal multi-gun walking fortress that
strides on legs, aims a dominant main cannon forward, works a side-mounted turret on
each flank, and sweeps a radar vane — as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only
the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete voxel cells** — one recorded
operation at a time — and you **author its walk and weapon animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Aegis is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the fortress into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every
form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking, firing fortress
needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log, in the shared volume's coordinates. The
volume starts **empty**. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no
transparency. Each call only records the operation to that part's log and renders
**nothing** — run `voxel-anim render` to (re)draw the previews and the assembled
scene (`--component <part>` redraws one part; `--time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at an instant of an animation), and you **must** run
`voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry the
run is scored from — an unrendered part is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name chassis
voxel-anim fill-box --part chassis --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their keyframes),
and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is
the authoritative contract. **Read `parts/<part>.png` after you run `voxel-anim render`** to see that
part's progress, **and read the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole
fortress composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene
views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together and that the legs reach the
ground. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each
part where it belongs on the finished fortress; the scene simply composes them there.

